* START-HISTORY:
* 10 Sep 26 dm  New, the port's !is_sd_user (14 Aug 2026) for Linux.  The port
*               reads back the Windows account description CREATE_USER stamps;
*               here CREATE_USER's useradd (through sd-elevate) stamps the GECOS
*               comment "SD account", and this reads it with getent, which
*               needs no privilege.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
* is_sd_user(username) - true when the Linux user exists and its GECOS comment
* is exactly "SD account".
*
* status() 0 - the user exists; the result says whether SD made it.
* status() 1 - no such user, a name that is not valid, or getent gave nothing
*              back.  The caller must not go on to delete anything.
*
* EXACT MATCH, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.  A comment somebody typed that merely
* contains the words is not SD's stamp, and the one caller deletes on true.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function is_sd_user(username)
$catalog !is_sd_user

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

sd_created = @false
set.status 0

if username = '' then
   set.status 1
   return sd_created
end

if not(valid_os_name(username)) then
   set.status 1
   return sd_created
end

gent.out = ''
os.execute 'getent passwd ' : username capturing gent.out

* getent prints one line, name:x:uid:gid:gecos:home:shell, and nothing at all
* (exit 2) for a user that does not exist.  The captured output arrives with
* its line break as a field mark, so the line is field 1.
gent.line = gent.out<1>
if gent.line = '' then
   set.status 1
   return sd_created
end

if field(gent.line, ':', 1) # username then
   set.status 1
   return sd_created
end

if field(gent.line, ':', 5) = 'SD account' then sd_created = @true

return sd_created
end

* END-CODE
